46
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:09,540
Celsius.

47
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:11,200
That's colder than anything you'll ever

48
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:12,260
find in a freezer.

49
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:13,860
Colder than the deepest winter in

50
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:14,780
Antarctica.

51
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:16,700
So in one planet, you have

52
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:17,780
a difference of more than 600

53
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:18,660
degrees.

54
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:20,060
That's not a weather change.

55
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:21,640
That's two different worlds sharing one

56
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:22,580
surface.

57
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:24,940
So the big question is this.

58
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:26,420
How can a planet so close

59
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:28,140
to the Sun get this cold?

60
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:30,580
The first answer is something you're

61
00:01:30,580 --> 00:01:31,860
breathing right now.

62
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:34,800
Air. Earth's atmosphere works like a

63
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:35,540
blanket.

64
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:36,760
It traps heat during the day

65
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:37,700
and keeps some of it through

66
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:38,360
the night.

67
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:39,560
That's why you can step outside

68
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:42,100
after sunset and not freeze instantly.

69
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:44,480
Mercury has almost no atmosphere.

70
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,180
Just a few scattered atoms, so

71
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:47,360
thin that they barely touch each

72
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:49,640
other. Scientists call it an exosphere,

73
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:50,820
and it's closer to empty space

74
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:51,900
than to air.

75
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:53,660
Why is it so thin?

76
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:55,100
Because Mercury is small, so its

77
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:56,380
gravity is weak.

78
00:01:56,380 --> 00:01:57,500
And because the Sun keeps blasting

79
00:01:57,500 --> 00:01:59,120
the planet with light and charged

80
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:00,760
particles that strip away any gas

81
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:02,000
trying to stay,

82
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,380
Mercury can't hold onto a blanket,

83
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:04,780
even if it wants to.

84
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,380
So what happens when the Sun's

85
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:07,780
heat hits the ground?

86
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:09,500
Nothing stops it from leaving.

87
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:11,120
The surface soaks up the heat

88
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:12,080
during the day.

89
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:13,860
Then, when night comes, all of

90
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:15,160
that heat shoots straight back into

91
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:16,800
space. No air to slow it

92
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:18,640
down. No clouds, no blanket.

93
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:19,740
Just rock in a very cold

94
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:21,520
sky. But wait, if heat escapes

95
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:23,280
that fast, shouldn't every planet without

96
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:24,660
air freeze at night?

97
00:02:24,660 --> 00:02:26,020
Yes, but Mercury has a second

98
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:27,420
problem. And this one is about

99
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:28,420
time.

100
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:30,100
Here's something that sounds fake, but

101
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:31,100
it's true.

102
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:32,720
Mercury goes around the Sun in

103
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:34,620
