104
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:35,880
That's its whole year.

105
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:37,300
But it spins very slowly.

106
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:39,060
One full spin takes about 59

107
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:40,300
Earth days.

108
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:41,800
Put those two together, and something

109
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:43,000
bizarre happens.

110
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,400
A single day on Mercury, from

111
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,060
one sunrise to the next, lasts

112
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:48,600
about 176 Earth days.

113
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:50,700
That's two full Mercury years, which

114
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:52,100
means the night is almost 88

115
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:53,820
Earth days long.

116
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:55,920
Almost three months of darkness.

117
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:57,520
Think about that.

118
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:58,840
On Earth, the night lasts maybe

119
00:02:58,840 --> 00:02:59,740
12 hours.

120
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,040
The ground barely has time to

121
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:01,760
cool.

122
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:03,240
On Mercury, the surface has nearly

123
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:04,620
three months to leak every bit

124
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:06,020
of its heat into space.

125
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:07,300
That's why the night side doesn't

126
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:08,320
